#{[17592186119571 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119571 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the area of a circle that has a radius of $\\sqrt 2$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119571 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57ebd9c4-2338-5beb-8b58-0902de3d7f33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119571 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the area of a circle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\pi r^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting the radius\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n&= π \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= π \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119572 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119572 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the area of a circle that has a diameter of $24$?\n\n![](images/9c/9cf9119f565f252c632c7fe2407d9f693847c72ef3b8fc3ddf03ffbf0cb3ce1b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119572 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57533c91-7a96-52c7-b9a4-fee398a961b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119572 #edb/ref 1074 "The radius of a circle is half the diameter. So, the radius of our circle is $r=12$.\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n&= π \\cdot 12^{2} \\\\\n&= 144π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119573 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3186" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119573 #edb/ref 1071 "The window in the attic of Mary's house has the shape of a quarter circle, as shown below. If the area of ​​the window is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\,\\textrm{m}^2$, what is the diameter of the corresponding circle?\n\n![](images/1f/1f22d702fbf4504b14787f71a45e3821cdac6d3a90acb40c1a49ce498d28f986.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41291efa-9dcd-5988-ac32-3a85f82bf5e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119573 #edb/ref 1074 "The area of the circle is four times the area of the quarter section. So,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=4\\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} =2\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nThe formula for the area of a circle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\pi r^2\n$$\n\n, where $r$ is the radius. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n2π &= πr^{2} \\\\\n2π &= πr^{2} \\\\\n2 &= r^{2} \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt{2}m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the diameter is\n\n$$\nd=2\\cdot r= 2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119676 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119676 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the area of a circle is $22\\pi\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$, what is its radius?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119676 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78a454c8-c08b-5c4a-8ff6-cd093296c6d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119676 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the area of a circle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\pi r^2\n$$\n\n, where $r$ is the radius. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n22π &= πr^{2} \\\\\n22π &= πr^{2} \\\\\nr^{2} &= 22 \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt{22}cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119710 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119710 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the volume of a rectangular solid that has length, width, and height of $5 \\: \\textrm{m}$, $\\sqrt3 \\: \\textrm{m}$, and $\\sqrt6 \\: \\textrm{m}$, respectively?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119710 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78d0bc32-ce61-5c58-a31a-bf3b0cc55761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119710 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nl=5 \\, \\textrm{m}, \\qquad w=\\sqrt3 \\, \\textrm{m}, \\qquad h=\\sqrt6 \\, \\textrm{m}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = 5 \\cdot \\sqrt3 \\cdot \\sqrt6 = 5\\sqrt{18} = 15\\sqrt2 \\: \\textrm{m}^3\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119711 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119711 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the height of the rectangular solid below given that its volume is $700\\:\\textrm{cm}^3$.\n\n![](images/8a/8a232f69c9c6abbffcb2272d4fbd237c644273826fb46dd5742bdc07d89544cb.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119711 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35043b46-40e9-51c4-8676-ad7a81763aae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119711 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nV = 700, \\qquad l=14, \\qquad w=10\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n700 &= 14 \\cdot 10 \\cdot h \\\\\n700 &= 140h \\\\\nh &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height is $5\\:\\textrm{cm}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119712 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119712 #edb/ref 1071 "The diagram below shows a carton of orange juice. One-third of the carton is filled with juice, forming a cube. Find the volume of the empty space in the carton.\n\n![](images/90/90280dd78d285df5dc798c3ec90b7fb47a72414f2934d36885eb9d65fd7584fd.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119712 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3528ee00-01a7-5c77-af81-50d53b5fa849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119712 #edb/ref 1074 "The orange juice in the carton creates a cube. Since the height of the juice is $9\\,\\textrm{cm}$, the side length of the cube is also $9\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\nThe height of $9 \\: \\textrm{cm}$ shown in the diagram represents a third of the carton's height. Therefore, the height of the carton is\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot 9 = 27 \\: \\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nThe empty space in the carton is a rectangular prism that is $9\\:\\textrm{cm}$ long, $9\\:\\textrm{cm}$ wide, and\n\n$$\n27 - 9 = 18\\:\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nhigh.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nl=9\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad w=9\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad h=18\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = 9 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 18 = 1\\,458\\:\\textrm{cm}^3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that there is $1\\,458 \\: \\textrm{cm}^3$ of empty space in the carton." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing Angles in the Coordinate Plane With the Unit Circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119751 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "24d0f296-256c-5da4-8992-e2112dfe99b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing Angles in Radians in the Coordinate Plane With the Unit Circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119752 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6435eb94-76f8-5e46-b005-03451ab6eba2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1039 "Using Trigonometry to Find an Angle in the Coordinate Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119753 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119569 #edb/ref 17592186045