49-aafa-c697c47d0110" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120591 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120592 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120592 #edb/ref 1061 10884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120592 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "86f2d07d-852b-50a6-a78c-64632ef6192f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120592 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120593 #edb/ref 1061 10886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120593 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "930eef61-6f72-50c9-aa7a-49a4a5916ac3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120593 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120760 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120760 #edb/ref 1061 25172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120760 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f5e37c48-c83d-50eb-8302-fa4de1388ce0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120760 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120806 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120806 #edb/ref 1061 33545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120806 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "974ddcf1-fc2c-505b-8c87-39b02be85d11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120806 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120807 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120807 #edb/ref 1061 33546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120807 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "43fe0233-b488-5e80-a3a1-01c13e10309c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120807 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120808 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120808 #edb/ref 1061 33547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120808 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3554cb95-0998-5f0a-af81-3cb3a1724059" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120808 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120809 #edb/ref 1061 33548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120809 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0abecfd9-a388-5a84-bdca-878bd2b73eb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120809 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121003 #edb/ref 1064 "The unit circle turns out to be a really useful tool to help us to understand the trigonometric functions. However, sometimes, we'd like to represent an angle in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle.\n\nThankfully, the standard way that we represent angles in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle is pretty much the same as with the unit circle!\n\nFor example, the angles $45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ$, and $315^\\circ$ can be represented in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n![](images/31/317690d0489b8a5f5cd9e360c9d5894a539908b00ffc50ae0b70bfe03708162b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121003 #edb/ref 1065 2410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121003 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "365c6b7d-1c9d-5476-a76c-1ae37c55c79d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121003 #edb/ref 1067 "Representing Angles in the Coordinate Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121004 #edb/ref 1064 "The **unit circle** is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/13/138322240b05231ce56ca195a06724da8a1c05ec4701ac1d9d49cadc9129c8c0.png)\n\nThe unit circle is an essential tool for understanding trigonometry.\n\nSuppose that we want to construct an angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane. There is a standard way in which we do this.\n\n- First, we draw the unit circle.\n- Then, we represent $\\theta$ as a central angle of the unit circle. The angle has its vertex at the origin, its initial side lies on the positive $x$-axis, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nFor example, we represent the angles\n\n$$\n\\theta = 45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ\n$$\n\nand $315^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d4419633933ba2b5874873f625ca9016d42c2dc9a685c9db3619daf8512fde5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121004 #edb/ref 1065 2411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121004 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e10ff02b-0d3a-58a5-813d-2072db6f706f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121004 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121005 #edb/ref 1064 "The area of a circle with radius $r$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\pi r^2\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the area of a circle that has a radius of $3\\textrm{cm}$.\n\n![](images/ea/ea7b4221ec972df5bce524c031ac82ecd7f427aaf6fd04ce2a93734d9597d271.png)\n\nUsing the area formula, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n&= π \\cdot (3)^{2} \\\\\n&= π \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 9πcm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121005 #edb/ref 1065 2424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121005 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "81aba826-d72c-50d9-85e5-cb77544b3df7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121005 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121085 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the rectangular prism below. How can we calculate its volume?\n\n![](images/94/94bdf8333e1cb6a7ff9f99bc4ef854433bc99a8310f61181309cf51709e7569c.png)\n\nIn general, we can find the volume $V$ of a rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nl = 20\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad w=10\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad h=5\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 20 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 5 = 1000cm^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121085 #edb/ref 1065 5377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121085 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "feec31b3-1ad3-5875-8ef1-2ea93c1a154f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121085 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124283 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124283 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\n![](images/6f/6fbded3e0a3faefda0e1f2c8418f5e271fb116cde4082487096378f89511b695.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124283 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "454d081a-fdcb-5101-9f85-0e3b27986695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124283 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n![](images/50/5036479a948abee5644cd3b96632f6aaf8ee370f7473ba27e11d25bb84ddb781.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- In graph IV, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph IV  shows a one-to-one function. This means that the function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II, and III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124284 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124284 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d9/d965e0b1087ad45206dd9259c963609f556bf5803af47ab18006bf40dffb2557.png)\n\n     \n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $(-3,-1]$\n\n- $(0,4]$\n\n- $[-2,2)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124284 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6722faa-6069-59c1-8861-75b8207b0a90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124284 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.   \n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-3,-1].$  Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/24/245484a6a77160a5f5892738a7e1f8e488f6df8e4ba990bbc94ae964c3305525.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $(0,4]$ and $[-2,2)$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/47/4737a1460e1abd4edc200404407fef10761f038d9a29e52ba0f8c3e8c69bb93f.png)\n\n          \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124285 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124285 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=|x-2|-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x \\in (-\\infty, 2]$\n\n- $x \\in (0, \\infty)$\n\n- $x \\in (-1,4]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124285 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c28a136-0b86-5d13-a5f8-b0dd0e0ea1ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124285 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's graph the given function. The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-2|-2$ can be obtained in the following way:  \n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right,  to get $y= |x-2|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y= |x-2|$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=|x-2|-2.$\n\n![](images/60/60c2601abfd3c4034991408f47b75c8cf70a1bccb72f73b2f9624a52d0ed7b42.png)\n\nNow, remember that a function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in(-\\infty,2].$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/45/45b0cdd26280d9379a449af41edbc9e69dd9d76f3b4116d938fbe708c21f6f29.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in(0,\\infty)$ and $x \\in (-1,4].$ On these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/55/55b8483c59362d3a59dfd269db74f2c5f17a97cee1f25c896ece16890d80bd42.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592