ef 17592186125324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1045 "Invertible Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7d6120fd-aa08-5562-9120-ce173e8fe311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1061 13908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5b61f81e-5366-54d7-b4dc-eb12246fa126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1061 13909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "619ae142-fadd-53d9-975f-a485d1f363b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1061 13911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "067f6188-2d0b-5a28-8513-b138cb5eda82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125325 #edb/ref 1061 13916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125325 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "956e822f-5a25-5d3b-ac84-ada9c2c8dee8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125325 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1064 "A function is **invertible** on an interval if and only if it's one-to-one. In other words, it must pass the horizontal line test.\n\nNot all functions have inverses! For example, consider the function $f(x)=x^2-1$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/e9/e99832fa1867a8314dda20f392d0c897ebb2942ac91a652ab1cf474b5d2d9612.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not a one-to-one since it does not satisfy the horizontal line test.\n\nTo find the graph of a function's inverse, we usually reflect it over the line $y=x.$ However, if we reflect $f(x)$ over the line $y=x,$ the result is not a function since it will not satisfy the vertical line test.\n\n![](images/7a/7a5a95f1cc3e2c8b40e865a24d22f4529e47c3c0137a6cb8d0369059f4a89a56.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is *not* invertible.\n\nIf a function is not invertible, we can sometimes make it invertible by restricting its domain.\n\nHere, if we take $f(x)=x^2-1$ over the restricted interval $x \\in (0,\\infty),$ then it passes the horizontal line test and we can find the corresponding inverse function by reflecting the graph of $y=f(x)$ over the line $y=x.$\n\n![](images/0a/0a7f98c2dce4599ee74cc13dc53c3a693b9c3716ffe54c708811f4c011f18b6d.png)\n\nTo determine if a function is invertible over a particular domain, we need to use the horizontal line test to check if it's a one-to-one function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1065 2768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "90b8b9a7-2b60-5a13-9829-c02575ad8c16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126206 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126206 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126206 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126206 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126206 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126206 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126206 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126206 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186126207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126206 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f9b62cac-9385-5bff-8c35-d1b52ef42ebf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126207 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126207 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126207 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a8bfb4dd-e556-5d6f-aaef-fddc9a598988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126208 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126208 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126208 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bee69923-eae2-5ddf-a42b-d1b058dc8af9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126209 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the statements are true" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126209 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126209 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "db97e4bf-99f0-504e-a725-2a9b37da3c66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126210 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126210 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126210 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "672fa625-0676-5877-87a0-a3ecd00928cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126211 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126211 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126211 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "767cf28e-15b6-599c-8613-52500710d20b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129054 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129054 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following expressions is irrational?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{3}{5} + 5^{9}$\n2. $7 + \\sqrt{5}$\n3. $\\sqrt{64} + 7^3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129054 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d6839658-bf90-55e5-845c-c3ded22c7735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129054 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- the sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the sum of two irrational numbers *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Expression I is not irrational. Since both $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ and $5^9$ are rational, their sum must be rational.\n- Expression II is irrational. Since $7$ is rational and $\\sqrt{5}$ is irrational, their sum must be irrational.\n- Expression III is not irrational. Notice that $\\sqrt{64} = 8$ is a rational number. The number $7^3$ is rational too. So, their sum must be rational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129055 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129055 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following expressions are irrational?\n\n1. $6^3 \\cdot \\sqrt{7}$\n2. $\\dfrac{1}{10} \\cdot \\sqrt{11}$\n3. $\\dfrac{1}{2^9} \\cdot \\sqrt{25}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129055 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bed9f966-a72e-56d4-acb9-3e156d763c96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129055 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that\n\n- the product of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Expression I is irrational. Since $6^3$ is rational and $\\sqrt{7}$ is irrational, their product must be irrational.\n- Expression II is irrational. Since $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ is rational and $\\sqrt{11}$ is irrational, their product must be irrational.\n- Expression III is rational. Notice that $\\sqrt{25} = 5$ is a rational number. The number $\\dfrac{1}{2^9}$ is rational too. So, their product must be rational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying a Sum or Difference of Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186129054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bbf0b9c3-84ba-51c8-bb7e-a45a441834ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129236 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129236 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129236 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129236 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying a Product or Quotient of Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129236 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186129055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129236 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "36b04804-a1f3-57de-b966-65074210fc6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1045 "Sums and Products of Rational and Irrational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "15f10ca2-71ff-5122-8f23-034d870b20ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130150 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130150 #edb/ref 1061 36260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130150 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37af3d14-d0da-5833-8cc2-8d34a27c4558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130150 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130151 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130151 #edb/ref 1061 36261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130151 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ae24fd7-3348-5c2c-97a8-bf4a352b05a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130151 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130152 #edb/ref 1061 36263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130152 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e1aa31d-da89-5630-8461-cb2becfe2266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130152 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130153 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130153 #edb/ref 1061 36266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130153 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1b0c4be-3561-5047-b474-fc789baf401e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130153 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130161 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130161 #edb/ref 1061 36625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130161 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "120df569-6a65-5f8d-9c7d-5fcfadd9b271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130161 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130162 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130162 #edb/ref 1061 36626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130162 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d13633da-c1a6-540e-a940-eff75190b892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130162 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130432 #edb/ref 1064 "When adding numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the sum is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational. Rational numbers can be represented as fractions of the form $\\dfrac ab$ (where $a$ and $b$ are integers), and adding two of these fractions always produces another fraction.\n- The sum of a rational and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$\\sqrt 2 + \\dfrac12$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the sum in decimal form, we get\n$1.414213562… + 0.5 = 1.914213562…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following sum is irrational:\n$\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt 3 = 3.146\\,264\\,37\\ldots$\nHowever, the following sum is rational:\n$\\sqrt 2 + (-\\sqrt 2) = 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130432 #edb/ref 1065 5941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130432 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1b2b00f9-12fd-5755-9340-1a9477f61cdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130432 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130433 #edb/ref 1064 "When multiplying real numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the product is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The product of two rational numbers is *always* rational since the result must be a fraction of the form $\\dfrac ab$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers.\n- The product of a *nonzero* rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$2\\cdot \\sqrt 2$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the product in decimal form, we get\n$2 \\cdot 1.414213562… = 2.828427125…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following product is irrational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{3}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 3}; = \\sqrt{6}$\nHowever, the following product is rational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 2}; = \\sqrt{2^{2}}; = 2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130433 #edb/ref 1065 5942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130433 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "74425e69-1e7a-5c1c-b1ac-2aec2e2ad1df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130433 #edb/ref 1067 "Products of Rational and Irrational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130437 #edb/ref 1064 "To summarize:\n\n- the sum (or difference) of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the sum (or difference) of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the sum (or difference) of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130437 #edb/ref 1065 5998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130437 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "df9f71cf-81c8-546c-99f3-cca45192f745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130437 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130438 #edb/ref 1064 "To summarize:\n\n- the product (or quotient) of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the product (or quotient) of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the product (or quotient) of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nFinally, it's worth pointing out that if $a$ is irrational, then the second rule implies that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1a\n$$\n\nis also irrational." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130438 #edb/ref 1065 5999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130438 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "45f6a1bf-6d91-5130-8aa5-a2d5863e2654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130438 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133546 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133546 #edb/ref 1071 "The quadrilateral $ABCD$ is rotated by an angle $\\alpha$ counterclockwise about the point $A$. The resulting polygon is $A'B'C'D'$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ coincides with $A'$\n2. $\\angle ACB \\cong \\angle A'C'B'$\n3. $AB = A'B'$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25f331e5-db8f-58bd-a4a6-24d7ed09db10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133546 #edb/ref 1074 "The given transformation is a rotation of $\\alpha$ counterclockwise about the point $A$, which is a rigid motion. Rigid motions preserve the distances between all points and all angle measures.\n\n![](images/73/73d6148ffe8d269f7846cf95679cddef30e058380aef6546fb8bd7577d9e9b80.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Under the action of the transformation, the point $A$ is left unchanged. Therefore, $A=A'$.\n- Statement II is true. Under the action of the transformation, the measures of all corresponding angles are preserved. Therefore, $\\angle{ACB}\\cong \\angle{A'C'B'}$.\n- Statement III is true. Under the action of the transformation, the distance between all corresponding points is preserved. Therefore, $AB = A'B'$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133547 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2907" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133547 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the polygons $\\mathcal{P}$, $\\mathcal{R}$, $\\mathcal{S}$ shown below can be obtained from the polygon $\\mathcal{Q}$ with rigid motions?\n\n![](images/ab/abd2df7fd01c1d0b04bb9b25a2df48738532d12cab70c1d24038a018e04dd2db.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46db11d7-8448-54f5-a5ea-46abfc746574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133547 #edb/ref 1074 "By transforming $\\mathcal{Q}$ using only rigid motions, we can get only polygons congruent to $\\mathcal{Q}$.\n\nSimilarly, any polygon congruent to $\\mathcal{Q}$ can be obtained from $\\mathcal{Q}$ using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{R}$ is not congruent to $\\mathcal{Q}$, it can't be obtained from $\\mathcal{Q}$ using rigid motions.\n\nThe polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{S}$ are congruent to $\\mathcal{Q}$. Therefore, we can obtain them by applying a combination of rigid motions to $\\mathcal{Q}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133548 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133548 #edb/ref 1071 "In terms of rigid motions, why are the polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ congruent?\n\n![](images/1d/1de244f4d4ad5ff86c6c300025edbc50e43415855487ae73e704478802e780e8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d928641d-fd70-5cbb-a217-e9e454a8bd51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133548 #edb/ref 1074 "The polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ are congruent because $\\mathcal{Q}$ is the image of $\\mathcal{P}$ under the action of rotation by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the origin.\n\n![](images/66/668fe5f1f5e0092a5ae6daceeada015ce6d84b25afea80570fea1aae318edc75.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133549 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133549 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following transformations map every polygon to a congruent polygon?\n\n1. $(x,y) \\mapsto (x-3, y-4)$\n2. $(x,y) \\mapsto (2x, 2y)$\n3. $(x,y) \\mapsto (y, x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133549 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fb05996-774b-5a89-9cc1-6876cc60338e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133549 #edb/ref 1074 "A rigid motion is a transformation that preserves distances. There are three types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n- Rotations\n- Reflections\n\nAlso, two polygons are congruent if and only if one can be mapped to another using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is a translation of $3$ units to the left and $4$ units down. This is a rigid motion. So, it maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n- Transformation II is a dilation of scale factor $2$. This is *not* a rigid motion. So, it does not map every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n- Transformation III is a reflection in the line $y=x$. This is a rigid motion. So, it maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133976 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133976 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133976 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements About Rigid Motions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133976 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133976 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "811ba45c-7467-5cef-9fb8-a8282891b607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Polygons That Map to Each Other Under Rigid Motions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cfe2598e-57bd-53fb-8477-9c4c5a8d3fa4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133978 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133978 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133978 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Why Polygons Are Congruent Using Rigid Motions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133978 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133978 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e4ffa315-2f50-51da-a928-329e1816706f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Maps Presented in Function Notation That Are Rigid Motions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b01281fd-5272-579c-98d5-ca5cc00c73e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186135006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186135007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186135008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186135009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186135010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1045 "Rigid Motions and Congruence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186135006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e6580470-6b81-57e7-bf68-123e2ad0a485" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134893 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186135007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134893 #edb/ref 1061 37342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134893 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6caa4091-01f6-57a9-8303-9c7ac2687088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134893 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134894 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186135008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134894 #edb/ref 1061 37367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134894 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b767960-73f6-5f1a-86d5-06e3a151b4d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134894 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135006 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135006 #edb/ref 1061 9555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135006 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "66e47972-9ac9-5e15-abc6-6b2f033b161a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135006 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135007 #edb/ref 1061 9556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135007 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "43999184-e956-5a86-8726-8697e9473d18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135007 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135008 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186135009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135008 #edb/ref 1061 9558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135008 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21aa14a0-3abb-5d4a-92b6-d08d24876eb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135008 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135009 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186135010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135009 #edb/ref 1061 9562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135009 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "971e2526-fd20-58e6-bc97-09ccc8b2617e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135009 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135010 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135010 #edb/ref 1061 9567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135010 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a91d4728-a024-5878-9599-76838230c6ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135010 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135021 #edb/ref 1064 "A **rigid motion** is any transformation that does not change the distances and angles between points. Rigid motions are also called **isometries**.\n\nThere are *only three* types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n- Rotations\n- Reflections\n\n**Watch out!** The following types of transformations are *not* rigid motions:\n\n- Dilations\n- Stretches" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135021 #edb/ref 1065 2294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135021 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9208af14-0cfd-5ca9-989f-3bf18330cf12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135021 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135147 #edb/ref 1064 "As we mentioned earlier, rigid motions are **distance-preserving**.\n\nFor example, if a rigid motion maps $\\triangle ABC$ to $\\triangle A'B'C'$, then the corresponding sides of the triangles must be congruent:\n\n$$\nAB = A'B', \\qquad AC = A'C', \\qquad BC = B'C'\n$$\n\nIn addition, rigid motions are **angle-preserving**, which means that the corresponding angles of the triangles must be congruent as well:\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = m\\angle A', \\qquad m\\angle B = m\\angle B', \\qquad m\\angle C = m\\angle C'\n$$\n\nSince all of the corresponding sides and angles are congruent, the triangles are congruent, and we write\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle A'B'C'\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135147 #edb/ref 1065 6157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135147 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4514dc0f-bb78-52d9-a728-8a09f7cbf667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135147 #edb/ref 1067 "Further Properties of Rigid Motions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135148 #edb/ref 1064 "So, remember, there are *only three* types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n- Rotations\n- Reflections\n\nThere are two properties of rigid motions we need to know:\n\n- *A rigid motion maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.*\n- *For any pair of congruent polygons, there exists a rigid motion that maps one polygon onto another.*\n\nConsider the diagram below. Let's use the concept of rigid motions to explain why $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ are congruent.\n\n![](images/04/0487c9bb2a640a44e9ee67830937db4272c97f4a5f5101e43e3dcf3edd6c3a1c.png)\n\nNotice that $\\mathcal{Q}$ is the image of $\\mathcal{P}$ under the action of reflection in the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/18/183b182e26df36c6cfe6a9d682e0f3b000bff40ce7d405255a0abfee8529a946.png)\n\nSince $\\mathcal P$ is mapped to $\\mathcal Q$ under a rigid motion (reflection), $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ must be congruent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135148 #edb/ref 1065 6163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135148 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "511ff35e-2666-5989-ad2f-e3e9fe541c7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135148 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of Rigid Motions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147226 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2175" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147226 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose fourth term is $2$ and whose seventh term is $-686$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147226 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d8383ad-c617-5b01-8055-214a8c639f12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147226 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n$, we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_7 = -686$ and $a_4 = 2$. Substituting $n=7$ and $m=4$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{7 - 4} &= \\frac{a_{7}}{a_{4}} \\\\\nr^{3} &= \\frac{-686}{2} \\\\\nr^{3} &= -343\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{3} &= -343 \\\\\nr^{3} &= (-7)^{3} \\\\\nr &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147229 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2178" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147229 #edb/ref 1071 "The third term of a geometric sequence is $80$ and its fifth term is $20$. Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147229 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7aa4b1ba-4362-5a4b-88d4-25d9ebe2cef7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147229 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n$, we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_5 = 20$ and $a_3 = 80$. Substituting $n=5$ and $m=3$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{5 - 3} &= \\frac{a_{5}}{a_{3}} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{20}{80} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\nr^{2} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147230 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2179" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147230 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation for the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose $4$th term is $6$ and whose $9$th term is $192$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "84364858-da8e-5241-85ca-ec84057bc6e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147230 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=4$ and $n=9$, respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{4} &= a_{1}r^{4 - 1} = a_{1}r^{3}, \\\\\na_{9} &= a_{1}r^{9 - 1} = a_{1}r^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $a_4 = 6$ and $a_9 = 192$, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{a_{1}r^{3} = 6; a_{1}r^{8} = 192\n$$\n\nFinally, by dividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{8}}{a_{1}r^{3}} &= \\frac{192}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{8}}{a_{1}r^{3}} &= \\frac{192}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{r^{8}}{r^{3}} &= 32 \\\\\nr^{5} &= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147290 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147290 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the name of the polyhedron shown below?\n\n![](images/b6/b6b4021c8add7d92bfbc98c23f9c9e56e4fd0c8fa152e60c04ca78c5c5b594cd.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147290 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "319d252c-a8c4-5a5f-ad2b-f4bdb5702b75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147290 #edb/ref 1074 "The polyhedron is a **heptagonal prism** since it has two identical heptagonal faces connected by equal parallel line segments.\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not an octagonal (pentagonal or hexagonal) prism since it does not have an octagonal (pentagonal or hexagonal) base.\n- The solid is not a cube since not all of its faces are squares." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147291 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2668" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147291 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is **not** a polyhedron?\n\n![](images/ce/ceedc03f96a3c801f31069f5551a8c0e5241dbabfc5c9463240a1eb1698a7105.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147291 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0583c82a-049c-51e6-aebc-bcd32423824e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147291 #edb/ref 1074 "Each face in a polyhedron must be a polygon. Therefore, the only option that is **not** a polyhedron is:\n\n![](images/ae/aea63ad8ff248347de606d37112355cdecc30ab5ae631b987b87f0b9f9d68f17.png)\n\nNotice that one of its faces is not a polygon (it has curved sides)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147294 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147294 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/be/beed0799de4f42cde58bf0c85f31a1a6850ef1227ea2827e9819a4edeb3ede4a.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?\n\n1. Hexagonal prism\n2. Hexagonal pyramid\n3. Rectangular prism" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147294 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc558592-e65f-5369-890d-fffc633f2a3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147294 #edb/ref 1074 "The solid is a **hexagonal pyramid** since its base is a hexagon and all other $6$ faces meet at a single point\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a hexagonal prism since it doesn't have two identical hexagonal faces connected by equal parallel lines.\n- The solid is not a rectangular prism since not all of its faces are rectangles." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147392 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8975" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147392 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the name of the solid shown below?\n\n![](images/43/4397eb1e78f99d0f6cda1515a4aef23d1a9e44235623c086f5ae849b4863fb45.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147392 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4447e034-350f-5e6b-a501-0f57a3888bd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147392 #edb/ref 1074 "The solid is a **right cone**.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The solid has one circular base and one vertex. So, it's a cone.\n- The axis that passes through the center of the circle and the vertex is perpendicular to the base. So, it's right." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Spheres, Hemispheres, Cones, and Cylinders" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f4196423-3401-54e1-b214-9dbc4861cfe1" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
